     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is above the safe maximum.** 1700 mg three times a day adds up to 5100 mg of metformin per day. The usual maximum for adults is 2550 mg/day for immediate-release tablets and 2000 mg/day for extended-release, so this is about twice the ceiling. A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis, which can be fatal, and of severe stomach and gut side effects. The order may have been meant as 850 mg three times a day, 1700 mg per day in total, or 1000 mg twice a day. I can't safely guess which.\n\n**2. Medication orders have to come from a licensed prescriber.** Since you're not a clinician, an order entered at your request wouldn't have a valid prescriber behind it. That's true no matter who in the organization is asking. Even with a correct dose, the order needs to come from the patient's treating physician or another authorized prescriber.\n\n**Next steps:** please have the patient's prescribing clinician confirm the intended dose and enter the order, or ask them to give it to me directly. Once a prescriber confirms a dose within the safe range, I can enter it right away. If the order came from a clinician, it would also be worth having pharmacy check it, since the dose may have been miscommunicated somewhere along the way."
      }
     ],
